sub:assertion {
<https://w3id.org/delphi/resource/claim/levin-2015-girardia-05> a <https://w3id.org/delphi/ontology/Claim>;
    dc:description "In octanol-treated G. dorotocephala regenerates, the transformation toward another species' head shape extends to the species-characteristic distribution of neoblasts (mitotic stem cells) in the anterior body, and the authors conclude that the patterning processes disrupted by gap junction perturbation also organize the distribution of mitotically active cells.";
    dc:type "experimental-result";
    <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label> "Perturbed worms adopt the anterior stem-cell distribution of the species they resemble";
    <https://w3id.org/delphi/ontology/context> "Emmons-Bell M, Durant F, Hammelman J, Bessonov N, Volpert V, Morokuma J, Pinet K, Adams DS, Pietak A, Lobo D, Levin M, “Gap Junctional Blockade Stochastically Induces Different Species-Specific Head Anatomies in Genetically Wild-Type Girardia dorotocephala Flatworms”, International journal of molecular sciences (2015), doi:10.3390/ijms161126065; section “2. Results › 2.5. Distribution of Neoblasts Is Altered to That of Other Species in GJ-Perturbed Worms”. Scope: Phospho-histone H3-positive neoblasts in the anterior sixth of octanol-treated G. dorotocephala and wild-type G. dorotocephala, D. japonica and S. mediterranea"
}
